{{Infobox galaxy|name=Aquarius ll|constellation name=[[Aquarius (constellation)|Aquarius]]|dist_ly=180,000 ly{{Contradictory inline|date=August 2025|reason=Distance given as 110,000 parsecs *and* 180,000 light years elsewhere, but a parsec is 3.26 light years, and those only differ by a factor of 1.6?}}|stars=|notes=Difficult detection|names=Aquarius 2, AQU 2, Aqu ll|size=160 pc}}

'''Aquarius ll''' (also '''Aquarius 2''', '''AQU 2''' or '''Aqu ll''') is a mildly elliptical dwarf [[satellite galaxy]] to the [[Milky Way]] located around 110 [[parsec|kiloparsecs]]{{Contradictory inline|date=August 2025|reason=Distance given as 110,000 parsecs *and* 180,000 light years elsewhere, but a parsec is 3.26 light years, and those only differ by a factor of 1.6?}} from [[Earth]] in the [[constellation]] of [[Aquarius (constellation)|Aquarius]]. It has a size of 160 [[parsec]]s. Aquarius ll is currently moving away from the [[Galactic Center|galactic center]] of the Milky Way. This galaxy contains many old [[red giant]] branch stars that are predominantly metal poor.<ref name=":0">{{Cite journal |last1=Torrealba |first1=G. |last2=Koposov |first2=S. E. |last3=Belokurov |first3=V. |last4=Irwin |first4=M. |last5=Collins |first5=M. |last6=Spencer |first6=M. |last7=Ibata |first7=R. |last8=Mateo |first8=M. |last9=Bonaca |first9=A. |last10=Jethwa |first10=P. |date=2016-11-21 |title=At the survey limits: discovery of the Aquarius 2 dwarf galaxy in the VST ATLAS and the SDSS data |journal=Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society |volume=463 |issue=1 |pages=712–722 |doi=10.1093/mnras/stw2051 |doi-access=free |issn=0035-8711|arxiv=1605.05338 }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Aquarius II |url=https://www.newplanetarium.com/milky-way/satellite-galaxies/aquarius-ii |access-date=2025-07-13 |website=New Planetarium |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Bruce |first1=Jordan |last2=Li |first2=Ting S. |last3=Pace |first3=Andrew B. |last4=Heiger |first4=Mairead |last5=Song |first5=Ying-Yi |last6=Simon |first6=Joshua D. |date=2023-06-01 |title=Spectroscopic Analysis of Milky Way Outer Halo Satellites: Aquarius II and Boötes II |journal=The Astrophysical Journal |volume=950 |issue=2 |pages=167 |doi=10.3847/1538-4357/acc943 |arxiv=2302.03708 |bibcode=2023ApJ...950..167B |doi-access=free |issn=0004-637X}}</ref>

It is located just 9 kiloparsecs from the [[Large Magellanic Cloud]] (LMC). Whether this is an association or coincidence is uncertain. Other dwarf galaxies discovered likely do have some association with the [[Magellanic Clouds]].<ref name=":0" />

== Discovery ==
It was discovered using the [[Very Large Telescope]] (VLT) and the [[Sloan Digital Sky Survey]] (SDSS). When it was found, it was initially identified as an over density of [[Red-giant branch|red giant branch stars]]. Detection of Aquarius ll was difficult as it lies close to the detection boundary of both [[luminosity]] and [[surface brightness]],which is why it was not identified earlier and making it one of the most difficult [[Dwarf galaxy|dwarf galaxies]] to discover.<ref name=":0" />

