{{Infobox galaxy|name=Tucana IV|constellation name=[[Tucana]]|type=[[Dwarf galaxy]]|notes=Close pass of the LMC|dist_ly=156,555 ly|group_cluster=Milky Way subgroup}}

'''Tucana IV''' is an ultra-faint [[dwarf galaxy]] that is a [[Satellite galaxy|satellite]] to the [[Milky Way|Milky Way galaxy]]. It lies at a distance of 48 kiloparsecs in the [[constellation]] of [[Tucana]]. It has a size of 127 parsecs.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Tucana IV |url=https://www.newplanetarium.com/milky-way/satellite-galaxies/tucana-iv |access-date=2025-07-28 |website=New Planetarium |language=en-US}}</ref>

It passed within 4 kiloparsecs (possibly a [[Galaxy collision|collision]] event) with the [[Large Magellanic Cloud]] (LMC) approximately 120 million years ago. This close passage of the LMC altered the trajectory of the galaxy and its internal [[kinematics]].<ref>{{Citation |last1=Simon |first1=J. D. |title=Birds of a Feather? Magellan/IMACS Spectroscopy of the Ultra-faint Satellites Grus II, Tucana IV, and Tucana V |date=2020-02-20 |url=http://arxiv.org/abs/1911.08493 |access-date=2025-07-28 |arxiv=1911.08493 |last2=Li |first2=T. S. |last3=Erkal |first3=D. |last4=Pace |first4=A. B. |last5=Drlica-Wagner |first5=A. |last6=James |first6=D. J. |last7=Marshall |first7=J. L. |last8=Bechtol |first8=K. |last9=Hansen |first9=T. |journal=The Astrophysical Journal |volume=892 |issue=2 |page=137 |doi=10.3847/1538-4357/ab7ccb |bibcode=2020ApJ...892..137S |doi-access=free }}</ref>

